 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. The compositing loop runs for each pixel, and for each sample, it processes all volumes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to the total size of the 3D textures for all volumes, limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` and `MaxMemoryFraction` [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance**: Performance depends on GPU hardware, resolution, and scene complexity. Adaptive sampling (`AutoAdjustSampleDistances`) can improve performance by reducing sample count in less complex areas [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling**: Scales with the number of volumes, but limited by `MAX_NUMBER_OF_ADDITIONAL_VOLUMES` (3-4) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Simultaneous rendering of multiple volumes, hardware acceleration via shaders, support for masking and adaptive sampling [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Limited number of additional volumes, potential performance overhead from `ReportProgress`, silent failure on invalid cropping [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use**: When multiple volumes need to be rendered together with accurate optical compositing, and hardware acceleration is available [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use**: When the number of volumes exceeds hardware limits, or when silent failures on invalid input are unacceptable [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of volumes. Adaptive sampling can reduce the number of samples based on scene complexity [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the total size of the 3D textures for all volumes, limited by `MaxMemoryInBytes` and `MaxMemoryFraction` [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Performance depends heavily on GPU hardware and the number of volumes. Rendering two volumes is supported natively; more volumes may hit hardware limits (e.g., `MAX_NUMBER_OF_ADDITIONAL_VOLUMES` is 3 on Apple, 4 on others) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales well with resolution if adaptive sampling is enabled. `ReportProgress` uses `glFinish()`, which blocks the GPU and can significantly slow down rendering of small datasets; it should be disabled for small datasets [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simultaneous rendering of multiple volumes, support for clipping/cropping, masking, and adaptive sampling. Uses GLSL shaders for broad hardware support [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited by hardware loop limits (requires nested while loops) and maximum number of additional volumes. Cannot handle extents starting from non-zero values without workaround [S2, unofficial]. Invalid cropping planes fail silently [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension of the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian to add support for multiple input datasets and properties [S1, unofficial]. The project uses GLSL shaders for GPU programming, chosen for their speed and broad device support compared to OpenCL [S1, unofficial]. It is used in scientific visualization and medical imaging applications where multiple datasets (e.g., different modalities or time steps) need to be visualized simultaneously [S1, unofficial].
+The GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper was developed as an extension of the Visualization Toolkit (VTK) to support multi-volume rendering on the GPU. It was modified by Carlos Falcón and Karl Krissian to extend the single-volume `vtkGPUVolumeRayCastMapper` to handle multiple datasets [S1, unofficial]. The project uses GLSL shaders instead of OpenCL for general GPU programming because shaders are faster and supported by more devices [S1, unofficial]. It is used in medical imaging and scientific visualization applications where multiple volumes (e.g., different tissue types or time steps) need to be visualized simultaneously [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
